{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792923603,"nonce":"qz+ggRatmYXRyndk3LDWRPlquGQ=","title":"LSP: declarations for the languages' own servers","message":"Part of 1626b90d930d209e848220743e00494408af449d2cdabdddea70f6c0a5337545 (caribou lsp).\n\nA developer keeps pyright, or another Python server, and lua-language-server\nfor their in-language features. Those servers report every import of a\nHaxe, Wren, plugin or other-language module as missing. caribou-lsp gives\nthem the project's modules in each language's own declaration form:\n\n- **Python.** A .pyi stub per module, under .caribou/stubs. Each stub holds\n  classes with typed methods and functions with typed parameters.\n- **Lua.** A LuaLS `---@meta` file per module, under .caribou/lua. Each\n  file holds classes, fields and functions with `---@param` and\n  `---@return`.\n- **Freshness.** The files are written from the registry and rewritten when\n  a module changes. They are a cache, not program source: never committed,\n  never read by the runtime.\n- **Types.** Registry types map to each language's annotations by the same\n  rules the bridge converts values by.\n\nDone when pyright and lua-language-server open the ChimpMonk example's\nguests with no unresolved import of a Caribou module.","files":null}]}